  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Between which two consecutive tenths does $\\sqrt[3]{45}$ lie?",
  "worked_solution": "To find the two consecutive tenths between which $\\sqrt[3]{45}$ lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose cubes bracket $45$.\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers $\\sqrt[3]{45}$ lies.\nWe know that ${3}^{3}=27$ and ${4}^{3}=64$.\nSince $27<45<64$, we know that $3<\\sqrt[3]{45}<4$.\n\nNext, we can test decimals between $3$ and $4$ to narrow down the interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3.4}^{3} & =39.304 \\\\ {3.5}^{3} & =42.875 \\\\ {3.6}^{3} & =46.656\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $42.875<45<46.656$, we can write:\n\n$$\n{3.5}^{3}<45<{3.6}^{3}\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives:\n\n$$\n3.5<\\sqrt[3]{45}<3.6\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt[3]{45}$ lies between $3.5$ and $3.6$.",
